        "title": "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "45b1f64f-9248-57a2-a310-050eb4c80ee5",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/c9/c9371d5e2bb921b4f5e58423bbce492f2b555122ee42bd861040b57d01070353.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-1\\le t<1,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=0.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8t}{2}=4t.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =4(0) \\\\  & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
